abstract: >-
  Blockchain consensus, rooted in the principle "don't trust, verify", limits
  access to real-world data that may be ambiguous or otherwise unverifiable by
  all participants. Oracles address this limitation by supplying data to
  blockchains, but existing solutions often compromise autonomy, transparency,
  or reintroduce trust. We propose Swarm Oracle, a decentralized network of
  autonomous robots (a robot swarm) that use onboard sensors and peer-to-peer
  communication to collectively verify real-world data and deliver it to a
  blockchain. Swarm Oracle leverages the built-in decentralization, mobility,
  and fault tolerance of robot swarms to respond flexibly to information
  requests, even in remote locations. Unlike typical cooperative robot swarms,
  Swarm Oracle integrates robots from multiple stakeholders, reducing dependence
  on any single party while introducing potential adversarial behavior among
  robots. We present a Byzantine fault-tolerant protocol that allows mutually
  untrusting robots to operate together and reach collective agreements of
  higher quality than individual estimates. Experiments with real and simulated
  robots show that consensus on uncertain environmental information can be
  achieved despite attacks by large proportions of the robots, and that a
  blockchain-based reputation system enables Swarm Oracle to autonomously
  recover from faults and attacks.
keywords:
  - "robot swarms"
  - "blockchain"
  - "oracles"
  - "Byzantine fault tolerance"
  - "multi-robot systems"
